ses we've run out  of reasons for an action and we just  have to cut carbon as quickly and as  dramatically as we can  um peaking of emissions allows us some  headroom because we are factoring in you  know the pent up demand from the  pandemic emissions have gone up last  year they might even go up this year but  essentially we've got another three  years where we have to stop increasing  the amount of carbon we put in the  atmosphere every year  and the context is very important  because  every year we put more carbon into the  atmosphere which is worsening the  climate crisis and the impacts that come  along with it you know your report  earlier was talking about extreme heat  flooding wildfires all of these are  increasing in severity and intensity  and frequency and they're affecting the  people that are contributing the least  to the problem i think that's a key  thing that the report also talks about  where the contribution uh to the problem  and and those who pay for it in terms of  lives and livelihoods are different sets  of people and it really bears a point  about responsibility and who owns  responsibility here and developed  countries uh clearly all responsibility  in terms of financing the transition for  a lot of the countries in the global  cell okay but also ensuring that we  model the transition here  julia tania says there that we're  running out of excuses and yet some  would say the war in ukraine  has put new pressures on people the the  soaring energy prices has put new  pressures on countries some of them are  having to turn to fossil fuels i mean  there are short-term problems that  countries are wrestling with aren't  there  but but surely the the best solution to  soaring energy prices is increasing  energy efficiency um using less energy  which is something you know which is  going to be fundamental to our um  our transition so this is the  opportunity to be supporting energy  efficiency to make sure people  understand what they can do to